WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:03.400
Zana · How to use a Squad

00:00:03.802 --> 00:00:08.480
Squads live in Settings, next to the
built-in ones you can launch right away.

00:00:08.663 --> 00:00:15.819
The quickest way to make your own is to just ask an agent.
Pick a project, describe the squad you want, and send it.

00:00:17.617 --> 00:00:24.261
The agent works out the roles, and writes the squad straight
into your project, as a file you can review and commit.

00:00:24.442 --> 00:00:28.297
Back in Settings, it's already
there, tagged with its project.

00:00:28.480 --> 00:00:35.681
You can also build one by hand. Click New squad, then
give it a name, an icon, and a short description.

00:00:35.862 --> 00:00:41.197
Add a slot for each role: an orchestrator,
two workers, and a code reviewer.

00:00:41.423 --> 00:00:46.843
Crown the orchestrator, so it starts first,
plans the work, and hands out the tasks.

00:00:47.025 --> 00:00:53.157
If you like, add an opening prompt, a default
project, worktrees, and launch approval.

00:00:53.339 --> 00:00:56.531
Save it, and it joins the catalogue with the others.

00:00:56.713 --> 00:01:04.402
Now let's launch it. Open New agent, switch to Squad,
describe the goal, and choose the squad and project.

00:01:06.249 --> 00:01:11.183
The orchestrator breaks the goal into tasks,
and the workers pick them up in parallel.

00:01:11.363 --> 00:01:15.565
Flow view shows who's working on
what, and how the work hands off.

00:01:15.746 --> 00:01:20.671
When the squad needs a decision, it doesn't
guess. It asks you, right in the Inbox.

00:01:20.852 --> 00:01:25.011
Answer once, and it carries on until every task is done.

00:01:26.801 --> 00:01:32.876
That's Squads. Ask an agent, or build
one yourself, then point it at any goal.
